# Matrix Traversals Cheatsheet

## Row by Row

The most basic traversal. Outer loop controls rows, inner loop controls columns. You visit every element left to right, top to bottom.

```cpp
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for(int j = 0; j < m; j++)
        // matrix[i][j]
```

## Column by Column

Same idea but flipped. Outer loop controls columns, inner loop controls rows. You visit top to bottom, then move to the next column.

```cpp
for(int j = 0; j < m; j++)
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        // matrix[i][j]
```

## Diagonal (top-left to bottom-right)

Each diagonal has a constant value of i - j. Start from the top row and left column. The key insight is that elements on the same diagonal share the same difference between row and column indices.

```cpp
// upper diagonals (starting from top row)
for(int d = 0; d < m; d++)
    for(int i = 0, j = d; i < n && j < m; i++, j++)
        // matrix[i][j]

// lower diagonals (starting from left column)
for(int d = 1; d < n; d++)
    for(int i = d, j = 0; i < n && j < m; i++, j++)
        // matrix[i][j]
```

## Anti-diagonal (top-right to bottom-left)

Each anti-diagonal has a constant value of i + j. Elements that sum to the same value are on the same anti-diagonal.

```cpp
for(int sum = 0; sum < n + m - 1; sum++)
    for(int i = max(0, sum - m + 1); i < min(n, sum + 1); i++)
        // matrix[i][sum - i]
```

## Spiral (outside to inside)

Shrink the boundaries as you complete each layer. You maintain 4 boundaries (top, bottom, left, right) and walk each edge in order: right across top, down right side, left across bottom, up left side. After each edge, shrink that boundary inward.

```cpp
int top = 0, bottom = n-1, left = 0, right = m-1;

while(top <= bottom && left <= right){
    // go right
    for(int j = left; j <= right; j++)
        // matrix[top][j]
    top++;

    // go down
    for(int i = top; i <= bottom; i++)
        // matrix[i][right]
    right--;

    // go left
    if(top <= bottom)
        for(int j = right; j >= left; j--)
            // matrix[bottom][j]
    bottom--;

    // go up
    if(left <= right)
        for(int i = bottom; i >= top; i--)
            // matrix[i][left]
    left++;
}
```

## Layer by Layer (for square matrices)

Used in rotation problems. You process concentric rectangular layers from outside in. The outer loop controls which layer, the inner loop processes elements in that layer.

```cpp
for(int layer = 0; layer < n/2; layer++)
    for(int j = layer; j < n-1-layer; j++)
        // process 4 elements per iteration using layer and j
```

The key: n/2 gives you the number of layers. For a 4x4 matrix you have 2 layers, for 5x5 you have 2 layers (center element stays).

## BFS/DFS on a Grid

Treat each cell as a node with 4 neighbors (up, down, left, right). This is the most important traversal for interview problems like flood fill, number of islands, shortest path in grid.

```cpp
int dirs[4][2] = {{0,1},{0,-1},{1,0},{-1,0}};

// BFS from a starting cell
queue<pair<int,int>> q;
q.push({start_i, start_j});
visited[start_i][start_j] = true;

while(!q.empty()){
    auto [i, j] = q.front();
    q.pop();
    for(auto [di, dj] : dirs){
        int ni = i + di, nj = j + dj;
        if(ni >= 0 && ni < n && nj >= 0 && nj < m && !visited[ni][nj]){
            visited[ni][nj] = true;
            q.push({ni, nj});
        }
    }
}
```

The direction array trick: instead of writing 4 separate if statements for up/down/left/right, you store the offsets in an array and loop through them. This is cleaner and scales to 8 directions (add diagonals) easily.

## Matrix Transformations

Three building blocks that combine to produce any rotation:

Transpose: swap rows and columns. Element (i,j) goes to (j,i).

```cpp
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for(int j = i; j < n; j++)
        swap(matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i]);
```

Reverse horizontal: reverse each row. Like putting a mirror on the right side.

```cpp
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    reverse(matrix[i].begin(), matrix[i].end());
```

Reverse vertical: reverse the order of rows. Like putting a mirror on the bottom.

```cpp
for(int i = 0; i < n/2; i++)
    swap(matrix[i], matrix[n-1-i]);
```

Combining them:

- Rotate 90 clockwise = transpose + reverse horizontal
- Rotate 90 counter-clockwise = transpose + reverse vertical
- Rotate 180 = reverse horizontal + reverse vertical

## Quick Reference

- Row/column: basic, use for simple scans
- Diagonal/anti-diagonal: elements share i-j or i+j
- Spiral: shrink 4 boundaries after each edge
- Layer: concentric rectangles, used for rotation
- BFS/DFS: treat grid as graph, use for connectivity and shortest path
